/-!
# The pointed endpoint-indexed path-class universal cover

Source: Hatcher, *Algebraic Topology*, pp.63–65, with covering uniqueness
Propositions 1.31/1.34, pp.61–62, and deck transformations, pp.70–71.
DAG node: `topo.pointed_path_class_universal_cover`.

The carrier, sheets, sheet-generated topology and prefix formula below are concrete
Mathlib data. The sigma carrier is not assigned a disjoint-union topology. Only the
named theorem's proof is postponed; no universal-cover predicate or undefined
construction is substituted for the path-class model.
-/

open scoped Topology _root_.Topology

namespace HolomorphicAnosov

noncomputable section
open Set Function Filter
open Path.Homotopic.Quotient

/-- The original path-class model gives the pointed universal cover and prefix deck action.

Source: Hatcher, *Algebraic Topology*, printed pp.63–65, especially the sheet
identity (*) on p.64 and stopped-path proof on p.65; covering path-class injection
Proposition 1.31 p.61, lift uniqueness Proposition 1.34 p.62, and deck transformations
Proposition 1.39 pp.70–71. This is the complete natural-language proof of DAG node
`topo.pointed_path_class_universal_cover`.

Semilocal simple connectedness is the displayed open-neighborhood condition on
loops based at the neighborhood center: their nullhomotopies are in X. It assumes
neither that those neighborhoods are simply connected nor that their loops contract
inside them. In the sheet definition the same ambient nullhomotopy condition is
required at every basepoint of the good open path-connected set. The deck action is
of the opposite of Mathlib's FundamentalGroup, so unop prefixing represents the
chronological loop class without any inversion.

Complete natural-language proof:

P1. Take E=Σ(x:X),Path.Homotopic.Quotient b x as an endpoint-indexed set. This sum is not given a
disjoint-sum topology and is not a compact-open path-space quotient: its topology will be
generated by the stipulated sheets. Its projection P(x,[α])=x and e₀=(b,[constant_b]) are
well-defined. Every class has a representative. Quotient composition and homotopy horizontal
composition ensure [αη] is independent of representatives, with its endpoint retained.

P2. Call U good when it is open, path-connected and all its loops are endpoint-fixed nullhomotopic
after inclusion into X. At x in any open O, semilocal simple connectedness gives a neighborhood N
of x whose loops based at x map trivially into X. First replace N by an open neighborhood inside
it, then use the open path-connected neighborhood basis to find x∈U⊆O∩N. For a loop λ in U based
at z, take a path ρ in U from x to z. The loop ρλρ⁻¹ lies in N and is nullhomotopic in X.
Conjugate this nullhomotopy by ρ⁻¹ and ρ, and cancel the reverse-forward pairs, to get λ
nullhomotopic at z. Thus U is good. Every path-connected open subset of a good U is good, since
any of its loops is already a loop in U. This proves the good sets form a basis in X. No local
simple connectedness or nullhomotopy staying inside U is assumed.

P3. For [α] ending in a good U, let S(U,[α]) be its stipulated sheet. Its endpoint map is onto U
because each y∈U can be joined to α(1) by a path η in U. If η and η′ both join α(1) to y, η
followed by η′⁻¹ is a loop in U and hence trivial in X. Right-compose its nullhomotopy with η′,
reassociate and cancel η′⁻¹η′; the constant-path laws give η~η′ relative endpoints. Left-compose
with α to obtain [αη]=[αη′]. Thus P restricted to S(U,[α]) is a bijection onto U. This uses
triviality of inclusion of loops, rather than the generally false claim that η~η′ within U.

P4. If [β]∈S(U,[α]), write [β]=[αη] with η in U. Every [βμ]=[α(ημ)] belongs to S(U,[α]).
Conversely [αμ]=[β(η⁻¹μ)] by associativity and forward-reverse cancellation (the cancellation leaf
applied to η⁻¹); the path η⁻¹μ stays in U. Therefore S(U,[β])=S(U,[α]). Every e∈E belongs to
S(U,e) for a good neighborhood U of P(e), using the constant path. If e belongs to S(U,a)∩S(V,c),
choose a good W with P(e)∈W⊆U∩V by P2. Then e∈S(W,e)⊆S(U,e)∩S(V,e)=S(U,a)∩S(V,c). Consequently the
sheet sets cover E and have the intersection-refinement property. Explicitly the family of their
unions contains ∅ and E, is closed under arbitrary unions, and is closed under finite
intersections by this refinement. It is a topology in which every sheet is open, and every
topology containing the sheets contains all their unions. Hence it is exactly the generated
topology specified in the statement, and sheets are a basis.

P5. P is continuous: for any open O⊆X and e∈P⁻¹(O), choose good V with P(e)∈V⊆O; S(V,e) is a
neighborhood of e contained in P⁻¹(O). For a fixed sheet S=S(U,a), P|S is the bijection of P3 and
is continuous. It is also open in the subspace topologies: given e in a relatively open A⊆S, the
sheet-basis refinement gives S(V,e)⊆A with V⊆U good; P(S(V,e))=V is open in U. Thus P(A) is the
union of these V. Its inverse is continuous, so P|S:S≃ₜU is a homeomorphism. This proves the
homeomorphism directly from the constructed topology, without presupposing a covering or invoking
lifting.

P6. For good U, the distinct sheets S(U,a) partition P⁻¹(U): every e above U lies in S(U,e), and
two sheets meeting at e both equal S(U,e) by P4. Each is open and maps homeomorphically onto U by
P5. This is an evenly covered neighborhood. Equivalently, fix x∈U and index sheets by P⁻¹({x}); P3
says each sheet contains exactly one such index. The inverse-image of U is homeomorphic to
U×P⁻¹({x}) with the index set discrete, by sending a point to its endpoint and sheet index; on
each open sheet this and its inverse are the homeomorphisms from P5, so the product maps are
continuous by open-sheet gluing. Therefore P is a covering. It is surjective since
path-connectedness of X supplies a path from b to any x. At e, every open neighborhood contains a
sheet S(U,e), homeomorphic to the path-connected good U, so E is locally path-connected by the
defining neighborhood criterion. E is also Hausdorff: different endpoint images have disjoint
inverse images of Hausdorff base neighborhoods; unequal points with the same endpoint lie in
distinct disjoint sheets over a common good U. This separation uses the stated Hausdorff
hypothesis, although covering construction itself does not need it.

P7. For α:b→x define q_t(s)=α(ts), so q_t=α.subpath(0,t), and Lα(t)=[q_t] in the endpoint-indexed
carrier. These are bona fide paths since scalar multiplication on the unit interval and α are
continuous. q_0 is constant_b and q_1=α. The stopped path α(min(s,t)) used by Hatcher has the same
class: the explicit homotopy α((1-r)min(s,t)+rts) is continuous, starts at α(min(s,t)), ends at
q_t, and fixes the endpoints b and α(t) for every homotopy time r. For continuity of Lα at t₀,
choose a good U about α(t₀); continuity of α gives a relatively open interval J in [0,1]
containing t₀, with α(J)⊆U. Shrink J to the intersection of [0,1] with an open real interval. For
any t∈J, the subpath α.subpath(t₀,t) stays in U, including when t<t₀. The arbitrary-parameter
subpath-composition homotopy gives [q_t]=[q_t₀ followed by α.subpath(t₀,t)]. Thus
Lα(J)⊆S(U,[q_t₀]) and Lα|J=(P|S(U,[q_t₀]))⁻¹∘α|J. The continuous inverse sheet map from P5 proves
continuity locally, hence at every t, including 0 and 1. This proves Lα continuous independently
of path-lift existence. Its endpoints are e₀ and [α], and P∘Lα=α, so these paths connect e₀ to
every point of E; reverse and concatenate them to connect any two points. Hence E is
path-connected.

P8. Let λ be an upstairs loop at e₀ and put α=P∘λ, a based loop at b by continuity of P. The
continuous Lα from P7 projects to α and starts at e₀. Apply the exact covering-path lift
characterization to both λ and Lα, only now that P is known to be a covering from P6. Both equal
the same canonical lift, so λ=Lα. Since λ(1)=e₀, we obtain [α]=Lα(1)=e₀=[constant_b] in
Path.Homotopic.Quotient b b. The injection of projected fixed-endpoint path classes through the
covering, with both upstairs endpoints e₀, reflects this equality to [λ]=[constant_e₀]. Quotient
equality means an endpoint-fixed nullhomotopy of λ. This establishes every loop at e₀
nullhomotopic without assuming E simply connected in any lifting premise.

P9. For a loop λ at an arbitrary e∈E, choose a path σ from e₀ to e using P7. The conjugate loop
σλσ⁻¹ at e₀ is nullhomotopic by P8. Compose this homotopy on the left with σ⁻¹ and on the right
with σ. Associativity, reverse-forward cancellation and constant-path homotopies reduce its
left-hand side to λ and its right-hand side to constant_e. Hence every loop at every point is
endpoint-fixed nullhomotopic. Apply the exact simply-connected loops criterion together with the
path-connectedness proved in P7 to obtain SimplyConnectedSpace E. This explicitly supplies the
all-basepoints quantifier of the criterion.

P10. For a based-loop class γ=[ℓ], define δγ(x,[α])=(x,[ℓα]). Quotient homotopy compatibility
proves well-definedness in both ℓ and α, with no topology on Γ required. Associativity and
constant-path homotopies give δγ∘δδ=δ_(γδ) and δ₁=id for chronological γδ (first γ then δ).
Reversal cancellation gives δ_(γ⁻¹) as a two-sided inverse. The literal sheet equation
δγ(S(U,[α]))=S(U,[ℓα]) follows by associativity; reverse inclusion follows by δ_(γ⁻¹). It gives
continuity of δγ and its inverse: inverse images of basic sheets are basic sheets. Thus δγ is a
homeomorphism and P∘δγ=P, so it is a deck homeomorphism.

P11. If δγ([α])=[α], right-compose with α⁻¹ and use associativity and forward-reverse cancellation
to get [ℓ]=[constant_b]; therefore γ=1 and the action is free. For [α],[β] over a common endpoint
x, take the based loop ℓ=β followed by α⁻¹. Then δ_[ℓ]([α])=[β(α⁻¹α)]=[β], using cancellation and
constant-path laws. Conversely prefixing a based loop preserves the endpoint. This proves exactly
P(e)=P(e′) iff e′=γ·e for some γ, without confusing deck-prefix action with path-lift monodromy.

P12. Mathlib multiplication is p*q=q.trans p. Set Γ=(FundamentalGroup X b)^op. If γ=op([ℓγ]) and
δ=op([ℓδ]), then unop(γδ)=unop δ*unop γ=[ℓγ.trans ℓδ]. Hence the chronological product in P10 is
literally the opposite-group product and its prefix action is a left action. The inverse is path
reversal and the unit is the constant class as already used. This uses the supplied multiplication
convention, with no extra inversion of loop classes.
-/
theorem pointed_path_class_universal_cover
    {X : Type*} [TopologicalSpace X] [T2Space X]
    [PathConnectedSpace X] [LocallyPathConnectedSpace X] (b : X)
    (hsemi : ∀ x : X, ∃ U : Set X, IsOpen U ∧ x ∈ U ∧
      ∀ p : Path x x, (∀ t, p t ∈ U) → p.Homotopic (Path.refl x)) :
    let E := Σ x : X, Path.Homotopic.Quotient b x
    let good : Set X → Prop := fun U ↦ IsOpen U ∧ IsPathConnected U ∧
      ∀ x : X, x ∈ U → ∀ p : Path x x,
        (∀ t, p t ∈ U) → p.Homotopic (Path.refl x)
    let sheet : Set X → E → Set E := fun U a ↦
      {e | ∃ η : Path a.1 e.1, (∀ t, η t ∈ U) ∧
        a.2.trans (Path.Homotopic.Quotient.mk η) = e.2}
    letI : TopologicalSpace E := TopologicalSpace.generateFrom
      {S | ∃ (U : Set X) (a : E), good U ∧ a.1 ∈ U ∧ S = sheet U a}
    let P : E → X := fun e ↦ e.1
    let e0 : E := ⟨b, Path.Homotopic.Quotient.refl b⟩
    let prefixAction : (FundamentalGroup X b)ᵐᵒᵖ → E → E := fun γ e ↦
      ⟨e.1, γ.unop.trans e.2⟩
    Function.Surjective P ∧ IsCoveringMap P ∧
      LocallyPathConnectedSpace E ∧ SimplyConnectedSpace E ∧ P e0 = b ∧
      ∃ action : (FundamentalGroup X b)ᵐᵒᵖ →* deck P,
        (∀ γ e, (action γ : E ≃ₜ E) e = prefixAction γ e) ∧
        (∀ γ e, prefixAction γ e = e → γ = 1) ∧
        (∀ e e' : E, P e = P e' ↔ ∃ γ, e' = prefixAction γ e) := by
  let _ := pathCoverTopology b
  refine ⟨endpoint_surjective b, endpoint_covering b hsemi,
    cover_locallyPathConnected b hsemi, cover_simplyConnected b hsemi, rfl,
    prefixDeckAction b, ?_, ?_, ?_⟩
  · intro γ e
    rfl
  · exact prefix_free
  · exact prefix_orbit


end
end HolomorphicAnosov
